+# MCU/SX130X sync loop recovery in Basic Station
+
+Wifx patch. Applies to Basic Station's radio time synchronisation loop, `ts_updateTimesync()`
+in `src/timesync.c`.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Why this patch exists
+
+### The lockup
+
+`ts_updateTimesync()` keeps one reference sample per txunit, `timesyncs[txunit]`, and
+measures every new sample against it. The reference is only advanced at the very end of the
+function, under the `done:` label. An excessive-drift verdict returns before ever reaching
+it.
+
+If the first pair of samples of a session is itself bad, the reference is poisoned and every
+sample that follows is measured against a wrong value. Nothing looks excessive because the
+clock is bad; everything looks excessive because the reference is. And the loop cannot leave
+that state, because leaving it requires the comparison that keeps failing.
+
+### The two signatures observed in the field
+
+**A one-off bad pairing.** A fixed error `E` in the first pair produces an apparent drift of
+`E/Δt`, which decays as `1/n` as the interval since that pair grows. It resolves on its own
+once it falls back under the threshold, but slowly. Measured on target across three
+consecutive restarts of the same gateway:
+
+| First verdict | Retries | Outcome |
+|---|---|---|
+| -547.7 ppm | 15 | resolved, about 15 s |
+| +215.5 ppm | 6 | resolved, about 6 s |
+| +24 740 ppm | 57 | still at +1918 ppm after about a minute, extrapolating past ten |
+
+**A sustained rate error.** A field unit showed the opposite behaviour: the mean MCU drift
+climbed from -872.7 ppm to -904.7 ppm between retries 3 and 18, increasing rather than
+decaying. That is not a bad pair, it is a real rate error between the two clocks, consistent
+with a time daemon still slewing `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` after boot. This signature never resolves
+on its own, because the apparent drift it produces does not decay towards the threshold.
+
+### Why the existing escape hatch cannot help
+
+After `2*QUICK_RETRIES` rejections the code resets `drift_thres` to `MAX_MCU_DRIFT_THRES`,
+100 ppm. That is already its value at initialisation. It cannot unstick a loop running at
+500, 900 or 24 000 ppm, three orders of magnitude above the ceiling it restores.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Why the lockup is not cosmetic
+
+`timesyncs[txunit]` is what `ts_xtime2ustime()` reads to produce `txjob->txtime`, and the
+radio is armed with `pkt_tx.count_us = txjob->xtime`. A poisoned reference therefore does
+not move the RF instant. It moves the station's belief about when that instant is due, in
+`s2e_nextTxAction()`. A pairing error `E` shifts that belief by `E`, in either direction,
+and both directions lose a downlink:
+
+- **`E` greater than `TX_AIM_GAP`** (20 ms on this platform; the 60 ms value applies only to
+  the `cisco` and `rpi64` builds). The "missed TX time" guard does not fire, since it only
+  compares against `TX_MIN_GAP`, 10 ms. No recalculation corrects it either, because that
+  only happens for jobs carrying a `gpstime` or on a txunit switch. The concentrator is
+  handed a trigger value already in its past: no error is reported, the frame simply never
+  goes out. The SX1302 trigger register is 32 bits of 32 MHz ticks, so the usable range of
+  `count_us` is $2^{27}$ µs, about 134 s, after which a value in the past becomes
+  indistinguishable from one far in the future.
+- **`E` below `-TXCHECK_FUDGE`** (5 ms). The frame is scheduled correctly and would go out
+  on time, but the `TXCHECK_FUDGE` health check runs before transmission starts,
+  `ral_txstatus()` reports `TX_SCHEDULED` rather than emitting, and the code abandons a
+  frame that was about to be sent correctly.
+
+Both paths fall through to `altTxTime()`, which is shifted by the same `E` and fails the
+same way. Class B has no alternative slot at all.
+
+A sustained rate error `r` compounds instead of staying fixed: after `t` seconds of lockup
+the belief error is `r*t`, which crosses `TX_AIM_GAP` in about 22 s at 900 ppm and keeps
+growing for as long as the lockup lasts.
+
+---
+
+## 3. What the patch does
+
+Every `REBASE_RETRIES` consecutive rejections, the reference is advanced to the current
+sample, unconditionally.
+
+`REBASE_RETRIES` is a `#define` in `timesync.c`, set to 3, next to `QUICK_RETRIES`. It is an
+internal safety net, not something a deployment should tune, so it is deliberately not a
+`CONF_PARAM`.
+
+### Why the trigger is blind
+
+It does not try to tell a bad pairing apart from a genuine excessive drift, for exactly the
+reason the old `drift_thres` escape hatch deliberately did not either: a poisoned reference
+never looks excessive against itself, so any condition that waits for a "better" sample to
+turn up can stall indefinitely. That is the failure being fixed. Blind and periodic is what
+guarantees termination whatever the cause.
+
+### Why 3
+
+`N` is sized against the sustained-rate-error case, not the one-off pairing error, because
+that is the regime with no natural exit. Requiring the error accumulated over the rebase
+window to stay inside `TXCHECK_FUDGE`:
+
+$$|r| \cdot N \cdot \frac{\text{TIMESYNC\_RADIO\_INTV}}{2} \le \text{TXCHECK\_FUDGE}$$
+
+At `N = 3`, `TIMESYNC_RADIO_INTV` of 2100 ms and `TXCHECK_FUDGE` of 5 ms, this tolerates a
+rate error up to 1587 ppm: a 44 % margin over the roughly 1100 ppm worst case attributable
+to time-daemon slewing during convergence. Sizing on the daemon's configured slew range
+rather than on the single sample observed in the field keeps that margin meaningful if the
+configuration varies.
+
+The one-off pairing case is a side benefit. The first sample after a poisoned reference
+arrives a full `TIMESYNC_RADIO_INTV` later and rejections then retry at half that, so the
+worst-case time to recovery becomes about 4.2 s, against the tens of minutes observed on
+target.
+
+### What is deliberately left alone
+
+**`excessive_drift_cnt` is not reset when rebasing.** Only the `% REBASE_RETRIES == 0` check
+fires, on the same counter that already gates the periodic `WARNING` every `QUICK_RETRIES`.
+The counter keeps meaning "how long has this been going on", the warning keeps its cadence,
+and the reset on an accepted sample is untouched.
+
+**The `drift_thres` reset stays.** It is not made redundant by the rebase: the two repair
+different causes of the same lockup. The rebase repairs a stale reference, every
+`REBASE_RETRIES` rejections. The reset repairs a `drift_thres` that has ratcheted down to an
+unworkably low value, visible on target as `threshold q90` readings of a few tens of ppm in
+the periodic `MCU/SX130X drift stats` line, every `2*QUICK_RETRIES` rejections. Both happen
+to fire together at `cnt=6`, which is convenient: refreshing the reference and loosening the
+threshold in the same call maximises the odds that the next sample is accepted.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Reading the logs
+
+Module `SYN`. The existing warning, emitted every `QUICK_RETRIES` rejections, gains two
+fields:
+
+```
+[SYN:WARN] Repeated excessive clock drifts between MCU/SX130X#0 (3 retries): -547.7ppm (threshold 100.0ppm) vs prev=-0.3ppm dt_last=2100.0ms
+```
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `-547.7ppm` | the drift of the current sample against the reference, as before |
+| `vs prev` | the drift of the current sample against the immediately preceding one |
+| `dt_last` | the interval between the current sample and the reference |
+
+The two new fields separate the signatures without further instrumentation:
+
+- **`vs prev` near zero while the main figure is large** - the reference is the poisoned
+  element. The main figure will decay as `dt_last` grows.
+- **both close to the same nonzero constant** - a sustained rate error. Neither will decay.
+
+In both cases the actual pairing or rate error in microseconds is the main figure multiplied
+by `dt_last`.
+
+`vs prev` comes from a second per-txunit snapshot, `prevSamples`, which tracks the last
+sample that passed the quality filter regardless of the reference and regardless of the
+excessive-drift verdict. It is diagnostics only: nothing in the loop's decisions reads it.
+It is reset in `ts_iniTimesync()` alongside `timesyncs`, since it is scoped to the same
+xtime session and comparing across sessions with different session encodings would be
+meaningless.
+
+---
+
+## 5. What this patch does not do
+
+**It does not address `syncQual_thres`.** Its initial value is `INT_MAX`, which means no
+quality rejection at all for the first 30 samples. That is a related but separate issue,
+targeting a different part of the same failure, and on the data available it would not by
+itself have prevented the worst of the three restarts observed here. It is left for a
+follow-up.
+
+**It does not change the drift statistics, the outlier rejection or the quality filter.**
+The rebase only advances the reference; the accepted-sample path is untouched.
